00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.680 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this year PC gamers have experienced the  new multiplayer phenomenon and player  unknowns battlegrounds there have also  been a few exclusives like Dawn of War 3  and rising storm 2 and 2 console  classics Bayonetta and vanquish hit the  PC this year in all their glory but  while those games have shined in their  own respect plenty of great games may  have flown under your radar so let's  roll through a few of our favorite games  on PC that you might have missed this  year and as always subscribe to our  channel here at GameSpot for all your  gaming coverage there been plenty of  super tough platformers in the vein of  2010 Super Meat Boy and like many of  them super ood bear resurrection is  happy to kill you over and over again  but it also gives your deaths of purpose  whenever you die you leave behind a  corpse which essentially serves to make  a given level easier it might block a  laser from hitting you cover up a set of  deadly spikes or give you a boost to  make it jump easier for an extra  challenge you're free to play without  the assistance of these corpses though  doing so will cause you to miss out on  some fun opportunities to see how they  can modify a level the game also  provides a steady stream of new  adversaries and mechanics from beginning  to end the sense of satisfaction it  provides when you're able to make it  through a particularly difficult section  is incredible  as a love letter to 90s first-person  shooters straped captures the frantic  pace and thrills of its reference  material from the retro visuals and  blocky blood splattering to the variety  of weapons and abundance of Secrets  stripe is still worth playing even after  the campaign's final encounter if you  ever make it that far  each run is a thrill as you violently  dance through the game's tall  procedurally generated levels and the  roguelike structure heightens the stakes  as you get further along strafe has its  own unique charm with comical  self-awareness and silly yet threatening  enemies but the soundtrack weaves all  the game's elements together to elevate  the experience beyond the standard  first-person shooter each level has its  own synthol an electronic rock track  that instills a sense of wonder inspires  you to fight through the hordes or  intensifies the feelings of danger or  often makes you feel all these things at  once  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  imagine a relatively quiet town in the  American Midwest populated by  anthropomorphic animals that's possum  Springs we're not in the woods takes  place and as May a cat human you  experience a readjustment to your  hometown after getting the boot from  college this comes in the form of a  side-scrolling adventure where  conversations character interactions and  minigames propel you through a  complicated and emotional tale of modern  young adulthood  every story beef hidden tidbit line of  dialogue and optional interaction  encourages exploring the beautifully  painted world of night in the woods the  game isn't for everyone but it hits  exactly what it aims for through the  uniquely charming artwork and quirky yet  emotive soundtrack the poignant  storytelling is wrapped in elements to  elevate it above many games this year  dream daddy at that dating simulator is  a visual novel published by the game  grumps in which you play as a dad who  romances other dads in your own town it  became an instant hit among dating sim  fans when it was released in July but  even if you don't really consider  yourself the type to date dads you got  to give it a shot it's filled with puns  and dad jokes but at its core there's an  incredibly heartwarming story about  raising a daughter on your own it's also  inclusive without feeling like the  diversity is just posturing sexuality  and gender issues are presented  effortlessly but for the most part the  dad's narrative arcs are focused on  fatherhood in that sense dream daddy is  widely relatable  [Music]  what remains of Edith Finch is a game in  the same vein as dear Esther and gone  home and it tells a hauntingly beautiful  tale through the generations as suppose  it cursed leads every family member to  die in unexpected and bizarre ways as  Edith Finch you explore the huge  mysterious family manner in which  gameplay vignettes unearth how your  relatives spent their last moments the  story is cryptic gripping and dark it's  full of unexpected twists and turns and  could only effectively be told through a  videogame considering you could beat it  under three hours there's little reason  to wait before playing  graceful explosion machine falls into  the category of frantic 2d spaceship  shooters but it's not quite the bullet  hell experience there aren't any dense  curtains of seemingly unavoidable  projectiles here rather than waiting for  wave after wave of enemies to come your  way you pilot a cute little spaceship  through corridors and face off against  swarms of enemies using both traditional  laser cannons and a small but varied  selection of special weapons dashing  through groups of enemies is an  empowering and useful tactic that calls  back to the feeling of resogun the trick  to graceful explosion machine is to  quickly juggle your various weapons  while also boosting through the groups  of increasingly stronger enemies and  whatsits under your skin the dance is an  adrenaline rush fight to survive and to  climb the leaderboards these are just a  few of the gems we've played on PC this  year so check out our written feature on  gamespot.com for all the games that we  highlighted what's your favorite PC game  this year that should get more love if  you got something to say hit up the  comments give this video a thumbs up and  subscribe to our channel you know the  deal and don't forget to watch our other  videos on the best games that you might  have missed on ps4 switch and Xbox one

